# HEADING OS private data overlay — gitignore

# Rebuildable associative-memory index (regenerated by memory-index.py).
.memory-index/
.memory-index-code/

# Secrets and runtime credentials — NEVER commit these.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.sessions/
*.session
cookies.json

# Local runtime state.
.data/
.state/
.logs/
.sync/

# The runtime store, one folder per service (scripts/utils/runtime_dir.py), at
# the `runtime_store:` the engine's default registry names (`runtime-junk`;
# a registry that moves the store moves these rules with it).
# Only what is REBUILDABLE is ignored: cache/ (sync dumps, scrape caches, the
# indexes) and tmp/ (scratch). state/ and logs/ stay tracked: a queue, a
# dead-letter entry, a disposition log, a cursor, an audit trail is durable
# operational state and its loss loses an instruction or a trail (HELM ruling,
# 2026-09-14, after outside review). That ruling states ONE of the two bases.
# HELM settled the second the same day: a path is ignored when EITHER it is
# rebuildable (durability) OR it carries operational content that must not
# reach the public engine (content), and the two are independent. Every entry
# below names its basis, because a forgotten rule must make a file more
# CLOSED and never more open, and each of these files really does get rebuilt,
# so prose alone reads as redundant to the next reader. Lock and atomic-write siblings are
# transient wherever they sit. _orphans/ entries keep the status they had at
# their old path; the ignored ones are listed by name.
# basis: durability (rebuildable or transient; none of the four carries
# anybody else's words, and that is why the basis is not content).
/runtime-junk/*/cache/
/runtime-junk/*/tmp/
/runtime-junk/**/*.lock
/runtime-junk/**/*.tmp

# Machine state a person set by hand (e.g. the registry name `ops-radar-state`).
# The records are tracked; only the transient siblings a writer leaves beside
# one are not: a `flock` sidecar (`ops-radar.py` writes `ack.json.lock`) carries
# no data. One rule over the whole tree rather than per writer, because naming
# sidecars one path at a time let a new shape reach the engine's push guard.
# basis: durability (transient; a lock restores itself on first use).
/system/state/**/*.lock
/system/state/**/*.tmp

# Content carve-outs from the tracked-`state/` ruling above. That ruling is
# about DURABILITY and it names the queue, the dead-letter entry and the
# disposition log by name. Each of those three was ignored at its OLD path for
# a different reason, written beside the rule it replaced: what the file
# CONTAINS. An Action Queue card the CEO has not approved carries the
# recipient, the subject and the whole drafted body, and `push-all` commits
# with `git add -A`, so one unreviewed commit puts un-sent mail into permanent
# history. That is not hypothetical: MEASURED in the live overlay's own rule
# block, one `GET /action-queue` over a torn file plus one `push-all` was
# already enough to do it once, which is why `**/.quarantine/` exists at all.
# Nothing reconciled the two reasons when the store was designed, so this
# fails closed: durability is restored to exactly what it was before the store
# existed (every path below was ignored at its old location), and no content
# that has not been approved for sending enters history on a ruling that never
# mentioned content. Re-tracking any of these is HELM's to decide WITH the
# content reason in front of it; deleting a line here is the whole change.
# basis: content (recipients, subjects and whole drafted bodies of cards the
# CEO has not approved; a failed send is kept whole).
/runtime-junk/action-queue/state/action-queue/queue.json
/runtime-junk/action-queue/state/action-queue/disposition-log.jsonl
/runtime-junk/*/state/dead-letter/
# Email-intelligence fetch state: per-machine, high-entropy Exchange message
# ids, and `run-*.json` carries raw email bodies. One service owns all of it
# since 2026-10-16: `state.json`, `_latest-fetch.json` and their lock
# (`scripts/record_readers/sources/inbox.py`) and the `/email-intel` SKILL's
# `run-*.json`, all under `runtime:email-intel/state`.
# basis: content (per-machine Exchange message ids; run-*.json carries raw
# email bodies).
/runtime-junk/email-intel/state/

# The crm-autolog outbound-mail trail. This one is not a content rule, it is a
# mechanical one: the trail was under `/.sync/`, which this file ignores, and
# the move lifts it into `logs/`, which the ruling tracks. The Steward VM's
# data clone is PULL-ONLY, and a daemon-written tracked file there dirties the
# tree and aborts the next `git pull --ff-only`. A run written against exactly
# that failure was parked in the 2026-09-21 test-suite split, so the ignore rule
# below is what prevents it now, and removing the rule breaks nothing visibly.
# A per-machine daemon audit trail is not shared durable state.
# basis: content (one JSONL line per outbound mail, recipient address and
# subject: `log_outbound` in scripts/utils/crm_autolog.py, MEASURED
# 2026-09-14). Its pull-only-clone reason is mechanical and additional; it
# does not replace this one.
/runtime-junk/sync-exchange/logs/

# The logs that left the engine checkout on 2026-09-25 (HOS-5 section 7) and are
# per-machine daemon or guard output rather than shared durable state, for the
# same pull-only-clone reason as the line above.
# basis: content. The overlay-write record names every path written into the
# overlay, by every process, and grows without bound by the operator's decision
# of 2026-09-22 ("record everything"); the fireside and Sentinel logs carry
# message metadata from the channels they watch.
/runtime-junk/overlay-guard/logs/
/runtime-junk/fireside/logs/
/runtime-junk/sentinel/logs/

# Quarantined wrecks at every depth, in the store as everywhere else. A reader
# that finds a state file unreadable moves it aside rather than letting the
# next write erase it, so the wreck inherits the live file's content -- draft
# bodies and recipients included -- under a name nobody ignored. The live
# overlay has carried this rule since 2026-08-29; the store shipped without it,
# so every NEWLY created overlay tracked its own wrecks.
# basis: content (a wreck is a byte copy of the live file that was torn, so
# it inherits whatever that file held).
**/.quarantine/

# basis: content (MEASURED 2026-09-14 in the live overlay, after the store's
# first commit tracked all of these). The handoff pointer under `.latest/` is a
# COPY of the newest entry in the handoff archive, rewritten on every compact,
# and `prompt.md` carries the operator's own prompt text verbatim; the archive
# itself is the record and stays tracked.
/runtime-junk/handoff/state/.latest/

# basis: durability (every _orphans/ entry below was ignored at its old
# path as rebuildable or scratch, and keeps that status here; an orphan
# whose basis were content would belong in the block above instead).
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/_tmp/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/browser/chrome-profile/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/clipboard/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/.tmp/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/operations/implement/_tmp/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/operations/email-intelligence/_extracted-threads.txt
/runtime-junk/_orphans/outputs/operations/zoom-cleanup/.profile/
/runtime-junk/_orphans/.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
